<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    Unit tests for PowerFramework.Shared.Containers, the two container primitives that AAP section
    0.2.1.3 Correction 2 pulled into scope: OrderedMap (from ws_objects/pfw.utility.container.pbl.src/
    n_map.sru, insertion ordered with a positional get(int)) and Vector (from n_vector.sru, the
    column expression calculation and recursion stack). Both are consumed in process by DataServices
    and by Persistence, so a defect here reaches two services; this project is the gate that stops it.

    HAND AUTHORED ON PURPOSE, NOT TEMPLATED. AAP section 0.5.2 records the measured finding that
    `dotnet new xunit3` does not exist in the pinned SDK and that `dotnet new xunit` scaffolds the
    xunit v2 line with an older test SDK and an older coverage collector. Regenerating this file from
    a template would silently downgrade the whole test stack, so it is written and maintained by hand.

    EVERYTHING COMMON IS INHERITED AND IS DELIBERATELY NOT RESTATED BELOW. MSBuild walks up to the
    repository root Directory.Build.props, which supplies TargetFramework (net10.0), LangVersion,
    Nullable, ImplicitUsings, TreatWarningsAsErrors, EnableNETAnalyzers, AnalysisLevel,
    ManagePackageVersionsCentrally, Deterministic, ContinuousIntegrationBuild and the shared assembly
    metadata. None of those appears here, and none is relaxed: warnings are errors in this test
    project exactly as they are in the library it tests, which is how AAP section 0.7.2 wants it
    ("application and test alike"). Restating one would open the seam through which a later edit
    diverges this project from its fifteen siblings. RootNamespace and AssemblyName are omitted for
    the same reason: the SDK derives both from this file's name, and that is already the wanted value.
    No Directory.Build.props is added under shared/ either, because MSBuild stops at the first one it
    finds and a local copy would shadow the root contract.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <!--
      MANDATORY. xunit.v3 generates the assembly entry point and the test host launches this project
      as a process, so a library output is a hard build failure, not a style preference (AAP sections
      0.5.2 and 0.4.2.8). Do not "tidy" this away: without it the build breaks or zero tests are
      discovered. It belongs to this test project alone and never to PowerFramework.Shared.Containers,
      whose own project file records that it stays a class library for exactly this reason.
    -->
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>

    <!-- A test project is never shipped as a NuGet package. -->
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Exactly four packages, every one VERSIONLESS: central package management is switched on, so the
    single source of truth for versions is the repository root Directory.Packages.props (xunit.v3
    3.2.2, xunit.runner.visualstudio 3.1.5, Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk 18.8.1, coverlet.collector 10.0.1).
    Adding a Version attribute here is an NU1008 build error and would defeat the mechanism that keeps
    the repository on one audited version set (AAP section 0.5.4.1).

    Nothing else is referenced. Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc.Testing is pinned centrally but is scoped to
    the four service test projects; PowerFramework.Shared.Containers is a pure Base Class Library
    substitution with no hosting model, no HTTP surface and no I/O, so an in process web host here
    would be unrequested scope. No assertion, mocking or auto fixture library is added either: AAP
    section 0.5.3 enumerates the deliberately excluded packages, and xunit.v3 already carries Assert.
  -->
  <ItemGroup Label="Test stack. Versions are pinned centrally in Directory.Packages.props.">
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk" />
    <PackageReference Include="xunit.v3" />
    <PackageReference Include="xunit.runner.visualstudio">
      <PrivateAssets>all</PrivateAssets>
      <IncludeAssets>runtime; build; native; contentfiles; analyzers; buildtransitive</IncludeAssets>
    </PackageReference>
    <!--
      coverlet.collector is the component that emits coverage.cobertura.xml, which AAP section 0.6.7
      identifies as the exact artifact the 80 percent line coverage gate (constraint C-H) is measured
      from, produced by running `dotnet test` for this project with the "XPlat Code Coverage" data
      collector in the Release configuration. Note that a bare `dotnet test` builds Debug even straight
      after a Release build, so the Release configuration has to be requested explicitly when Release
      coverage is wanted. Dropping or mis-scoping this reference does not fail the build; it silently
      disables the gate, which is the worst of the available failure modes.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="coverlet.collector">
      <PrivateAssets>all</PrivateAssets>
      <IncludeAssets>runtime; build; native; contentfiles; analyzers; buildtransitive</IncludeAssets>
    </PackageReference>
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    Exactly one ProjectReference, which is what makes PowerFramework.Shared.Containers.OrderedMap and
    PowerFramework.Shared.Containers.Vector visible here. It satisfies constraint C-I, since this
    project restores, builds and tests from a clean checkout using nothing beyond the two root props
    files, global.json and this reference: no container, no daemon, no database, no network, no test to
    test reference and no path outside the repository. It also satisfies C-A and C-D, because it
    reaches no other shared library, nothing under services/ and nothing belonging to a deferred
    capability. No local solution file accompanies it: AAP section 0.3.2.1 records that .slnf filters
    are incompatible with the .slnx format and fail with MSB4014, and per service solutions live only
    under services/, so this project is enumerated by the repository root PowerFramework.slnx instead.
  -->
  <ItemGroup Label="System under test. One project reference, by design.">
    <ProjectReference Include="..\PowerFramework.Shared.Containers\PowerFramework.Shared.Containers.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    No Compile, Content, None or EmbeddedResource item appears anywhere above. The SDK globs the
    sibling *.cs test sources automatically, and this project has no data input of any kind, so
    nothing here reads, copies or embeds any part of the read only legacy tree (ws_objects/**, *.pbl,
    *.pbt or any root *.dll). That tree is the behavioural oracle these tests are written against and
    is never a build input, which is constraint C-C.
  -->

</Project>
